Source: ananicy-rs
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Maintainer: ananicy-rs packagers <ananicy-rs-packaging@example.invalid>
# ananicy-rs is a cargo workspace whose four crates are all unpublished, so
# `dh-cargo` and `debcargo` do not apply and this uses Debian's own cargo
# wrapper instead; both pcre2-sys and ananicy-platform link a system library that
# the default feature set needs. clang, libbpf-dev and rustfmt are absent because
# the `bpf` feature is not enabled.
#
# `make` is absent too: `make install` is what places the binary and the unit,
# but `make` is part of `build-essential`, which dpkg treats as an implicit build
# dependency and checks all the same. lintian says so too
# (build-depends-on-build-essential-package-without-using-version).
#
# `pkgconf` rather than `pkg-config`: the latter has been a transitional package
# since bookworm. It provides the same `pkg-config` binary, which is what
# pcre2-sys invokes.
#
# rustc is 1.88, not 1.85, and that is not a guess: edition 2024 needs 1.85, but
# the workspace uses let chains (`&& let`, 30 of them) and those stabilised in
# 1.88, so 1.85 parses every manifest and then fails on the code with E0658. No
# manifest carries a `rust-version`, so cargo cannot report this itself. Debian
# stable ships rustc 1.85.1, which is too old; see the note on the target suite
# in README.md.
#
# This field has no comments inside it on purpose. dpkg-source reads a
# continuation line that starts with `#` as a dependency, and says so:
# "cannot parse dependency # ...".
Build-Depends:
 debhelper-compat (= 13),
 cargo,
 rustc (>= 1.88),
 pkgconf,
 libpcre2-dev,
 libsystemd-dev,
Standards-Version: 4.7.0
Homepage: https://github.com/JohnRTitor/ananicy-rs
# The build only runs `cargo build` and `cargo test`, and neither needs
# privileges for this package's feature set. The tests in ananicy-platform that
# do need root detect that they are unprivileged and skip themselves with a
# message, which is the same thing mock and makepkg do by construction.
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: ananicy-rs
Architecture: linux-any
# The daemon is built on netlink, cgroups and procfs, and nothing in the tree
# compiles on another operating system.
#
# ${shlibs:Depends} already carries the two libraries the linker records:
# libsystemd.so.0 and libpcre2-8.so.0. Nothing depends on systemd as the init
# system, and nothing depends on a rule set: ananicy-rs ships no rules, and no
# Debian derivative packages ananicy-cpp or the Ananicy rule set, so a
# Recommends would name a package that cannot exist. See README.md.
Depends:
 ${shlibs:Depends},
 ${misc:Depends},
Description: auto-nice daemon that applies per-application process priorities
 ananicy-rs is a rewrite of the ananicy daemon in Rust. It watches process
 creation and automatically applies the nice value, I/O priority, scheduling
 policy, CPU affinity, OOM score adjustment and cgroup placement that a matching
 rule asks for.
 .
 It reads its rules from /etc/ananicy.d and ships none of its own, so a rule set
 has to be installed there before the service is useful. On first start the
 daemon creates /etc/ananicy.d if it is missing and writes a default
 ananicy.conf into it; neither is owned by this package, so upgrades never touch
 an administrator's configuration.
 .
 This build uses the default features: the netlink process-event source and
 systemd integration. The optional eBPF event source is not built; see
 /usr/share/doc/ananicy-rs/README.Debian.
